  "youtube": {
    "title": "Ohio and Michigan went to war over Toledo",
    "description": "In 1835, Ohio and the Michigan Territory both claimed the Toledo Strip, 468 square miles along their border, because the boundary set in 1787 relied on maps that put the southern tip of Lake Michigan too far north. Both sides called out their militias, but no one was killed. Congress then made giving up the strip the price of statehood, and in exchange Michigan received about 9,000 square miles of the western Upper Peninsula, where valuable copper and iron ore were later found. Michigan became a state on January 26, 1837.\n\nSources: Wikipedia, \"Toledo War\"; History.com, \"The Toledo War: When Michigan and Ohio Nearly Came to Blows\"; Michigan Department of Military and Veterans Affairs, \"The Toledo War\"; Michigan State University Department of Geography, \"The Toledo War\"; American Battlefield Trust, \"The War that Sparked a Sports Rivalry\"; Michiganology, \"The Toledo War\".\nImages: public domain. Portraits of Robert Lucas (John H. Witt) and Stevens T. Mason (Thomas Mickell Burnham, 1837, Detroit Institute of Arts) and John Mitchell's 1755 map of North America, via Wikimedia Commons. Quincy Mine and Calumet & Hecla Mine photos (Detroit Publishing Co., 1906, Library of Congress). Maps: Natural Earth (public domain).\n\n#history #shorts #michigan",
    "tags": [
